From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Eugene Feinberg <uge@persimmons.ai>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 20:07:09 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 01/37] LLHD LowerProcesses: lower processes that carry outputs
 as loop block args

llhd-lower-processes only converted a process to llhd.combinational when the
entry block and the wait block converge on the same block with equivalent
arguments. Older Verilog -- always @(*) with case statements assigning to regs
-- instead produces a loop header whose BLOCK ARGUMENTS carry the outputs:

    ^bb0: cf.br ^bb1(%c0, %false : i66, i1)   // pre-first-event values
    ^bb1(%v0: i66, %v1: i1):
      llhd.wait yield (%v0, %v1), (%a, %b), ^bb2
    ^bb2: ... cf.br ^bb1(%computed0, %computed1)

matchControlFlow()'s skipToMergePoint refuses to cross a branch carrying
destination operands, so entry stops at ^bb1 while the wait stops at ^bb2 and
the two never converge. On OpenC910 this rejected 912 of 1441 processes.

Such a process is still a function of its inputs: the entry block only
supplies the outputs' value before the first input event, and every path
through the body branches back with freshly computed values. Lower it by
making the wait's destination the entry block, dropping the initialisation
path. Where the destination itself takes arguments, prepend a block that
branches to it with the wait's destination operands.

Guarded by canReRootAtWaitDest(), which requires: the loop-carried arguments
feed nothing but the wait (if the body read them, a path branching back
without reassigning would preserve the old value -- a latch, i.e. real state,
which must not be lowered this way); any destination operands are defined
outside the process, so a synthetic entry can supply them; and the entry block
holds nothing but its branch, since it is about to become unreachable.

Dropping the initialisation path changes the value yielded before the first
input event from the entry constants to f(inputs). For synthesis that is the
correct reading, and it matches Verilog, where always @(*) does execute at
time zero.

OpenC910: 1441 -> 191 processes, residual llhd 10374 -> 1334. The 191 that
remain fail a different, legitimate check (unobserved value: incomplete
sensitivity lists in the RTL), not this one. ibex is unaffected -- its
pipeline output is byte-identical (netlist 4662b4c0cd) and it still lowers to
zero residual llhd.
